# MS2 — the second POSITIVE CONTROL, on the tenancy leg rather than the posture leg.
#
# `using (... or true)` is deliberately NOT `using (true)`: posture rule 8 rejects a
# policy whose rendered qual is literally 'true' or omits require_user_id, and this one
# fails neither test. The mutation therefore survives the posture gate and has to be
# caught by a tenancy test instead, which is what makes it a control for the SENTINEL
# mechanism (canon 8.1, V18) rather than for rule 8.
#
# Both named catchers must fail: the sentinel-visibility test and the cross-user read.
# Either one alone would leave the other free to rot.
#
# Expect a wide blast radius — around eighteen tests go red. That is informative rather
# than a problem: attribution here is by NAME, so a broad failure is still pinned to the
# two assertions that are supposed to notice.
# ATTRIBUTED, NOT ASSUMED. This mutation was MISATTRIBUTED by the gate before this note
# existed: the suite reported exit=1 with tests=0 passed=0 failed=0, so the two test
# catchers it named could not possibly have failed. Reproducing it in a scratch mirror
# gave the real cause, which is better news than a test failure:
#
#   20261021140600_sentinels.sql:252  ERROR: SENTINEL PAIRED CONTROL FAILED
#
# The sentinel migration seeds one sentinel per protected table and then asserts, as a
# PAIRED control, that a non-owner sees 0 and the owner sees 1. Weakening the tenancy
# policy to `... or true` makes everyone see everything, the control fires, and the
# migration ABORTS. The database is never built, so no test ever runs.
#
# A migration-time control that refuses to build a wrong database is strictly stronger
# than a test that notices afterwards, so the correct expectation here is the abort, not
# a test name. `#abort` says that explicitly.
#
# NOTE A GAP THIS LEAVES, deliberately recorded rather than hidden: because the build
# stops first, MS2 no longer demonstrates that the integration SUITE would catch a
# wide-open tenancy policy. That was verified separately by hand, by applying the
# weakening AFTER the migrations ran so only the suite could notice, across all eight
# forced-RLS policies, each caught. Registering a post-build variant would make that
# permanent and is the obvious next addition here.
id: MS2
title: decision tenancy USING weakened to `... or true`, keeping rule 8 satisfied
expect: caught
expect-test: #abort
proves: the sentinel rows make a cross-user READ denial non-vacuous (canon 8.1, V18)
proves: a tenancy regression that survives the posture check is still caught by a test

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140300_decision.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
create policy decision_tenancy on public.decision
  using (user_id = auth.uid())
@@REPLACE
create policy decision_tenancy on public.decision
  using (user_id = auth.uid() or true)
@@END
